1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,240
Welcome to this new in-depth analysis.

2
00:00:02,240 --> 00:00:07,200
Today we start with a question that seems almost like a paradox, and it is: do financial markets

3
00:00:07,200 --> 00:00:08,480
love war?

4
00:00:08,480 --> 00:00:13,320
To understand this, let's analyze a very interesting economic paper by Massimo Guidolin

5
00:00:13,320 --> 00:00:17,600
and Eliana La Ferrara, entitled precisely "Diamonds are a war's best friend,"

6
00:00:17,600 --> 00:00:22,440
it is a fascinating analysis that will radically change the way we think about the relationship

7
00:00:22,440 --> 00:00:25,080
between the private sector and geopolitical instability.

8
00:00:25,080 --> 00:00:27,480
So, let's get right into it.

9
00:00:27,480 --> 00:00:29,960
Here is the map of our analysis today.

10
00:00:29,960 --> 00:00:32,600
First, we will explore the paradox of peace.

11
00:00:32,600 --> 00:00:35,640
And then dive into the diamond market in Angola.

12
00:00:35,640 --> 00:00:40,960
We will then analyze the market reaction, the four economic causes behind it,

13
00:00:40,960 --> 00:00:44,000
and finally the consequences of all this.

14
00:00:44,000 --> 00:00:46,679
Point one: the paradox of peace.

15
00:00:46,679 --> 00:00:50,280
Or, in other words, does peace always equal profit?

16
00:00:50,280 --> 00:00:51,880
Imagine the situation.

17
00:00:51,880 --> 00:00:56,159
Normally, we take it for granted that peace is great for business,

18
00:00:56,160 --> 00:01:00,800
after all, war destroys infrastructure and terrifies investors.

19
00:01:00,800 --> 00:01:03,680
But this brings us to today's central question.

20
00:01:03,680 --> 00:01:08,120
What happens to the stock prices of mining companies if a civil war ends

21
00:01:08,120 --> 00:01:09,120
all of a sudden?

22
00:01:09,120 --> 00:01:14,560
Logic would suggest an immediate market euphoria, everyone buys and stocks

23
00:01:14,560 --> 00:01:15,560
soar.

24
00:01:15,560 --> 00:01:20,040
And yet, hold on tight, the data tells us a completely different story.

25
00:01:20,040 --> 00:01:25,400
And indeed, while our instinct tells us that prices should rise thanks to

26
00:01:25,400 --> 00:01:30,800
the rediscovered stability, the real market reaction recorded in Angola in 2002

27
00:01:30,800 --> 00:01:35,800
shows literally the opposite: prices collapse, a real plunge.

28
00:01:35,800 --> 00:01:39,960
This makes us realize that, well, economic theory often has to deal with a reality

29
00:01:39,960 --> 00:01:42,439
on the ground that is much, much more cynical.

30
00:01:42,439 --> 00:01:48,200
Let's move on to the second point, our case study: the diamond market in Angola.

31
00:01:48,200 --> 00:01:52,280
To understand this strange anomaly, we have to take a step back and immerse ourselves

32
00:01:52,280 --> 00:01:54,960
in the context of this complex civil war.

33
00:01:55,000 --> 00:02:01,720
In 1992, national elections are held; in 1994, the Lusaka Protocol is signed.

34
00:02:01,720 --> 00:02:02,960
It seems like a done deal, right?

35
00:02:02,960 --> 00:02:08,520
But instead, in 1998, the peace process collapses inexorably, and fighting resumes.

36
00:02:08,520 --> 00:02:10,920
Then, suddenly, the turning point.

37
00:02:10,920 --> 00:02:16,240
On February 22, 2002, Jonas Savimbi, the charismatic leader of the rebel

38
00:02:16,240 --> 00:02:17,240
movement, dies in an ambush.

39
00:02:17,240 --> 00:02:22,120
His passing, in fact, marks the sudden and unexpected end of the conflict, wiping out

40
00:02:22,120 --> 00:02:26,960
any uncertainty about the country's future—a perfect event to understand the market's reaction.

41
00:02:26,960 --> 00:02:29,879
And here comes the genius of the researchers.

42
00:02:29,879 --> 00:02:34,680
To financially isolate the impact of this sudden peace, the study

43
00:02:34,680 --> 00:02:37,680
uses a very rigorous comparative method.

44
00:02:37,680 --> 00:02:39,080
They created two groups.

45
00:02:39,080 --> 00:02:44,200
On one hand, an Angolan portfolio, which includes seven diamond companies already operating

46
00:02:44,200 --> 00:02:45,599
in the territory.

47
00:02:45,599 --> 00:02:50,680
On the other, a control portfolio made up of 42 companies identical in every way,

48
00:02:50,680 --> 00:02:53,200
but with no interests in Angola whatsoever.

49
00:02:53,200 --> 00:02:54,719
What is the purpose of this?

50
00:02:54,719 --> 00:02:58,920
Simple: to rule out that price changes are due to normal fluctuations

51
00:02:58,920 --> 00:03:00,800
in the global diamond market.

52
00:03:00,800 --> 00:03:05,960
Which brings us to the third point of our analysis: the market's reaction to the announcement

53
00:03:05,960 --> 00:03:06,960
of peace.

54
00:03:06,960 --> 00:03:09,439
So, what was the response on the stock market?

55
00:03:09,439 --> 00:03:13,879
If we look at the raw data, just as reported in table one of the paper,

56
00:03:13,879 --> 00:03:15,200
we notice something incredible.

57
00:03:15,760 --> 00:03:19,640
On the exact days Savimbi dies, the portfolio of companies operating in

58
00:03:19,640 --> 00:03:22,640
Angola records a dramatic net drop.

59
00:03:22,640 --> 00:03:27,640
On the contrary, or rather, almost in mockery of the situation, the control portfolio

60
00:03:27,640 --> 00:03:29,120
records gains.

61
00:03:29,120 --> 00:03:33,280
In practice, markets interpreted the end of the war as terrible news for

62
00:03:33,280 --> 00:03:34,280
those doing business.

63
00:03:34,280 --> 00:03:35,280
27.

64
00:03:35,280 --> 00:03:41,440
27 percentage points—this is the huge net difference in return between the control group

65
00:03:41,439 --> 00:03:45,800
and the Angolan portfolio, recorded on the very day the news became

66
00:03:45,800 --> 00:03:46,800
public.

67
00:03:46,800 --> 00:03:51,159
A single figure that throws in our face how much the end of the war damaged

68
00:03:51,159 --> 00:03:53,400
the perceived value of those companies.

69
00:03:53,400 --> 00:03:55,199
We are at the fourth point.

70
00:03:55,199 --> 00:03:57,960
The economic causes behind this crash.

71
00:03:57,960 --> 00:04:02,599
Naturally, the question arises: why on earth does all this happen? The researchers didn't

72
00:04:02,599 --> 00:04:07,680
just observe the data, but isolated four structural reasons: one,

73
00:04:07,680 --> 00:04:13,120
the collapse of entry barriers; second, the increase in government power; third, the

74
00:04:13,120 --> 00:04:18,079
end of a price war; and four, new transparency standards. Let's look at them one

75
00:04:18,079 --> 00:04:19,079
by one.

76
00:04:19,079 --> 00:04:20,079
First factor.

77
00:04:20,079 --> 00:04:26,519
As bad as it sounds, civil war acts as a giant barrier to entry.

78
00:04:26,519 --> 00:04:31,759
Operating in a conflict zone requires crazy investments in private security and a risk

79
00:04:31,759 --> 00:04:34,680
tolerance that few companies possess.

80
00:04:34,680 --> 00:04:37,439
This protects the companies that are already there.

81
00:04:37,439 --> 00:04:42,000
A sudden peace, however, breaks down this barrier and heralds the arrival of an avalanche

82
00:04:42,000 --> 00:04:47,120
of new competitors ready to take their slice of the pie in a now peaceful country.

83
00:04:47,120 --> 00:04:50,120
Let's move on to the second cause: the balance of power.

84
00:04:50,120 --> 00:04:54,639
During the war, the government must compete with the rebels for control of the territory,

85
00:04:54,639 --> 00:04:58,879
but once the conflict is over, the state regains an absolute and total monopoly over

86
00:04:58,879 --> 00:04:59,879
mining rights.

87
00:04:59,879 --> 00:05:03,839
And for foreign companies, this is a cold shower: there is a concrete fear

88
00:05:03,840 --> 00:05:08,340
that the government, now unrivaled, will impose exorbitant licensing fees, or decide

    89
00:05:08,340 --> 00:05:12,040
to unilaterally renegotiate old contracts in its favor.

90
00:05:12,040 --> 00:05:17,240
Third point, and this is perhaps the crudest: the end of the so-called price war.

91
00:05:17,240 --> 00:05:21,920
Peace permanently closes the doors on the black market run by rebels, who in the past

92
00:05:21,920 --> 00:05:26,160
sold off diamond-mining rights simply because they were desperate and needed

93
00:05:26,160 --> 00:05:28,680
money to buy weapons quickly.

94
00:05:28,680 --> 00:05:34,000
Just imagine that before, some operators paid ridiculous sums, sometimes as low as 250 dollars,

95
00:05:34,000 --> 00:05:36,840
to obtain massive prospecting rights from the rebels.

96
00:05:36,840 --> 00:05:40,240
With peace, these nightmare discounts disappear completely.

97
00:05:40,240 --> 00:05:46,840
Fourth and final factor: transparency. Peace inevitably brings the attention of the

98
00:05:46,840 --> 00:05:51,680
international community to the country. As long as there is war, the state of emergency is

99
00:05:51,680 --> 00:05:56,840
the perfect excuse for not having transparent finances, creating an ideal ecosystem for shady

100
00:05:56,840 --> 00:05:59,800
yet enormously profitable business.

101
00:05:59,800 --> 00:06:04,720
With the end of hostilities, international pressure kicks in to regularize the sector

102
00:06:04,720 --> 00:06:06,520
and goodbye to gray areas.

103
00:06:06,520 --> 00:06:11,440
And to add some color and human context to all these numbers, there is a quote

104
00:06:11,440 --> 00:06:16,600
by the Angolan journalist and dissident Rafael Marques, which hits right to the point.

105
00:06:16,600 --> 00:06:20,880
"The end of the war in Angola means that at this moment, the main institution

106
00:06:20,880 --> 00:06:22,920
of the country is corruption."

107
00:06:22,920 --> 00:06:27,720
A sharp sentence that reminds us exactly what kind of regulatory and ethical swamp

108
00:06:27,720 --> 00:06:31,240
these corporate dynamics operated in the post-war period.

109
00:06:31,240 --> 00:06:36,680
Here we are at the fifth and final point: the consequences of this discovery.

110
00:06:36,680 --> 00:06:40,879
Reaching the end of this analysis means having to come to terms with a deeply

uncomfortable reality.

Think about it: if the large companies already operating in a conflict-devastated country
obtain tangible financial benefits from the continuation of the war, what real
incentive will they ever have to use their huge political influence to promote
peace?

It is a heavy question, I realize that, but it is a question that forces us to look
beyond the surface and to fully understand the dark and fragile dynamics that control
the resources of our planet.